# Animations

## Related Rules

- `performance.md` for animation cost and keeping the JS thread free.
- `ui-design-system.md` for theme tokens (durations and easings are **not** theme values — they live in `LAYOUT_PRESETS`).
- `native-ios.md` for the New Architecture context this whole file exists because of.

## The one rule

**Do not add `LayoutAnimation.configureNext` to new code.** It does nothing on iOS.
Reach for `LayoutTransition` or `SlideTransition` from `src/components/transitions/` instead.

```js
// ❌ BAD — a no-op on iOS, so the change just snaps
LayoutAnimation.configureNext(LayoutAnimation.Presets.easeInEaseOut)
this.setState({ expanded: true })

// ✅ GOOD
<LayoutTransition>
  <Text numberOfLines={expanded ? null : 3}>{body}</Text>
</LayoutTransition>
```

## Why it is off

`index.ios.js:30` calls `LayoutAnimation.setEnabled(false)` on iOS at startup, before
anything renders (PROD-5801).

RN 0.87 routes `LayoutAnimation` through Fabric's `LayoutAnimationKeyFrameManager`,
which **withholds mount mutations and replays them** when the animation finishes. With
view flattening the replayed indices no longer match the shadow tree, so the next
unmount lands on the wrong child and the app aborts:

```
*** Assertion failure in -[RCTViewComponentView unmountChildComponentView:index:]
Attempt to unmount a view which has a different index.
  index: 16, actual index: 14, tag at index: 224
```

It crashed on **every signed-in launch**, which made the upgrade unusable for any
authenticated user.

Turning it back on is not available from this repo, and this was verified rather than
assumed:

- React core ships **prebuilt** on iOS (`React-Core-prebuilt`, `React.xcframework`), so
  the renderer that does the withholding cannot be patched here.
- The native `enableLayoutAnimationsOnIOS` flag defaults true and is what routes the API
  into that renderer — the only lever it offers is off, which is where we already are.
- The zIndex flips were ruled out as a cause (both Dashboard overlays pinned to a
  constant zIndex → identical crash).

`setEnabled` itself needed a one-line fix, the `LayoutAnimation.js` hunk of
`patches/react-native+0.87.1.patch`: its body in 0.87.1 reads
`isLayoutAnimationEnabled = isLayoutAnimationEnabled`, assigning the flag to itself and
ignoring the argument.

## This is scaffolding, not the target state

`src/components/transitions/` exists **only** because `LayoutAnimation` is disabled. It is
not a better API we chose; it is a workaround for a renderer bug we cannot reach.

Upstream **[facebook/react-native#57454](https://github.com/facebook/react-native/issues/57454)** is open. When it lands and
`configureNext` works under Fabric again, the intended end state is:

1. Remove the `setEnabled(false)` call and the `LayoutAnimation.js` hunk of the
   `react-native` patch. Keep the rest of that file: its codegen hunk (PROD-6855) has its
   own removal trigger, the upgrade to RN 0.88.
2. Convert the `LayoutTransition` / `SlideTransition` sites back to `configureNext`.
3. Delete `src/components/transitions/`.

Two consequences for how you use it **today**:

- **Do not spread it further than the motion requires.** A screen that reads fine
  snapping does not need a wrapper. Every site added is a site to convert back.
- **Do not build new abstractions on top of it.** Don't re-export it, don't wrap it in a
  helper, don't make it a prop on a shared component. Call sites should be trivially
  greppable and trivially reversible.

## `LayoutTransition` — expand / collapse / reveal

Animates its **own height** as content grows or shrinks. That it drives real layout is the
point: everything below follows on its own, which is what `configureNext` did to the whole
commit and why a purely visual transform does not reproduce the feel.

| Prop | Default | |
| -- | -- | -- |
| `children` | `null` | content whose height changes |
| `preset` | `easeInEaseOut` | see `LAYOUT_PRESETS` below |
| `duration` | preset's | override in ms |
| `enabled` | `true` | false adopts each height instantly (reduced motion) |
| `manual` | `false` | animate only changes armed through the ref — see below |
| `style` | `null` | applied to the animated container |

`LAYOUT_PRESETS` mirrors RN's own numbers plus this app's `src/cst.js` configs, so a
converted call site keeps its timing: `easeInEaseOut` 300, `linear` 500, `spring` 700,
`mediumAnimation` 700, `conversationAnimation` 700, `springAnimation` 750,
`defaultAnimation` 1000.

### `manual` + ref is the direct `configureNext` analogue

This is the pattern to reach for when the old code called `configureNext` immediately
before a `setState` / hook setter.

By default the component animates **every** height change — an answer arriving from the
network, text reflowing at a larger font size, a rotation. A card that animates when
nobody touched it reads as a glitch.

`manual` inverts that: nothing animates until a call site **arms** the next change,
exactly the way `configureNext` armed the next commit. Arming is one-shot — the change
that consumes it disarms it again.

**Arm it where the old `configureNext` call sat: immediately before the state update.**

```js
// Function component — SettingsFaqItem.js
const transition = useRef(null)

const handlePress = () => {
  transition.current?.configureNext()
  onPress()
}

<LayoutTransition manual ref={transition}>
```

```js
// Class component — PricingIntroOffer.js
this.plansTransition = React.createRef()          // constructor
this.plansTransition.current?.configureNext()      // before the setState
<LayoutTransition manual ref={this.plansTransition}>
```

Optionally pass `{ preset, duration }` to `configureNext()` to override for that one
change.

**Keep the `LayoutAnimation.configureNext` call next to it.** It is dead on iOS but still
works on Android, and dropping it regresses Android. Both lines together is the correct
shape:

```js
// PaywallFaqList.js
LayoutAnimation.configureNext(LayoutAnimation.Presets.easeInEaseOut)  // Android
rowTransitions.current[index]?.configureNext()                        // iOS
setOpenIndex(isOpen ? null : index)
```

**In a list, arm every row whose height changes in that commit — not just the one tapped.**
An accordion closes one row while opening another, so both move:

```js
rowTransitions.current[index]?.configureNext()
if (openIndex !== null && openIndex !== index) {
  // Otherwise the closing row snaps shut beneath the opening one.
  rowTransitions.current[openIndex]?.configureNext()
}
```

Collect the refs with a setter factory rather than one ref per row:

```js
const rowTransitions = useRef([])
const setRowTransition = (index) => (node) => {
  rowTransitions.current[index] = node
}
```

Note `PaywallFaqList` animates **each row** rather than the card around them: animating
the card would leave the rows at their final positions, jumping under a clip.

Use `manual` whenever the content can change for reasons other than the user's own action.
Leave it off only when every height change is one the user asked for.

### Respect reduced motion

Someone who has turned on Reduce Motion should get the new state instantly, not a slower
version of the animation. Two ways, both fine:

```js
// Skip the arming — PaywallFaqList.js
const { reduceMotion } = useReduceMotion()

if (!reduceMotion) {
  LayoutAnimation.configureNext(LayoutAnimation.Presets.easeInEaseOut)
  rowTransitions.current[index]?.configureNext()
}
setOpenIndex(isOpen ? null : index)
```

```js
// Or pass it down — the component adopts each height instantly when false
<LayoutTransition enabled={!reduceMotion}>
```

Prefer skipping the arming on a `manual` site (the state update still happens, it just
lands in one frame) and `enabled` on an automatic one.

## `SlideTransition` — sheets

For an always-mounted overlay that used `configureNext` plus a `top` toggle between `0`
and the screen height. Drives `translateY` on the **native driver**, so it mutates no
layout and the renderer is not involved.

```js
<SlideTransition
  visible={this.state.showCameraOptions}
  distance={cst.deviceHeight}
  style={{ position: 'absolute', top: 0, left: 0, right: 0 }}>
```

`distance` is required. `fade` also cross-fades, for the sheets that toggled opacity.

**Children stay mounted in both states, deliberately.** A sheet that unmounts while
presented is what aborts Fabric, and it leaves nothing to animate on the way out. Hidden
means `pointerEvents="none"` plus a transform parking it off-screen, so it cannot take
touches.

## Reanimated is not the answer here

`performance.md` says to prefer `react-native-reanimated` for frequent animations. That
holds for transforms and gestures. It does **not** hold for layout animations on this
stack:

- Reanimated's `LinearTransition` runs through `LayoutAnimationsProxy_Legacy`, the second
  documented root cause of this upgrade's "different index" aborts, and it is unpatched
  here (PROD-5826). Routing hundreds of sites through it to fix a crash mitigation is the
  wrong trade.
- Measured on this stack, Reanimated's layout animations **never run** at all.
- `entering` and `layout` are index-safe by construction; **`exiting` is not** — it is the
  one trigger that withholds Remove and Delete, the same withhold-and-replay shape that
  caused the crash. Never use `exiting`, and never on a list row.
- `Animated.FlatList` with `itemLayoutAnimation` stays off the table: it wraps every cell
  in an extra animated view, changing view flattening, which is exactly what the Legacy
  proxy does not model.

## Gotchas

**React 19 ignores `defaultProps` on function components.** Both components use default
parameters instead. This is not stylistic — it silently turned `enabled` into `undefined`
and made `LayoutTransition` apply every height instantly rather than animating it. If you
write a new animated component, use default parameters.

**Height cannot use the native driver.** `LayoutTransition` passes
`useNativeDriver: false` on purpose — a native-driven transform would not move the
siblings.

**The wrapper is only transparent inside a column.** It sets `alignSelf: stretch` and
takes the parent's width. Dropping it into a flex row will change the layout. This is why
the remaining sites were inventoried rather than bulk-wrapped.

**The first layout pass adopts, it does not animate.** A first render must not animate up
from zero, so the component carries no height until it has measured once.

## The ~1000 dead calls

There are still **1008 `LayoutAnimation.configureNext` call sites across 383 files**. They
are all no-ops on iOS and all still live on Android.

- **Do not bulk-remove them.** Android keeps its behaviour from them.
- **Do not bulk-wrap them either.** PROD-5953 inventoried 195 further candidate sites
  across 92 files; wrapping blind breaks layouts (see the flex-row gotcha).
- When you convert a site, **leave its `configureNext` call in place** — that is what the
  existing conversions did, so Android is unaffected.

Restoration is tracked under PROD-5803 and is explicitly **not a release blocker**: zero
of the call sites pass an `onAnimationDidEnd` callback, so nothing waits on a layout
animation before unmounting, navigating or firing a callback. The whole surface is
cosmetic.

## Don't

- Don't add `LayoutAnimation.configureNext` to new iOS code paths.
- Don't remove the `setEnabled(false)` call or the `LayoutAnimation.js` hunk of the
  `react-native` patch without the upstream fix — both are required, and the app crashes
  on every signed-in launch without them.
- Don't use Reanimated `exiting`, or any Reanimated layout animation, on this stack.
- Don't wrap a site in `LayoutTransition` unless the motion is actually wanted there.
- Don't put transition durations in the theme — they live in `LAYOUT_PRESETS` so a
  converted site keeps the timing its `configureNext` call had.
